designs.rationale:
I followed the Addendum K template: one main configuration, six single-parameter neighbours, everything logged, in-sample only with frames cut while read, declared.json with its sha256 before any return, then the attack and one separately declared look.

The main rule takes the choice most sources share at each step. The window opens after the 17:00 New York rollover; most vendors trade between 17:00 and 20:00 New York, and the core is the first hour. The first 15 minutes are left out because spreads jump then. Bars are M5, the most common timeframe. The bands are BB(20, 2.0): no two sources agree on their settings, so the indicator's standard values are used. A bar that closes beyond a band is faded at market on the next M1 open, which is the lab's convention. The target is the middle band, the most common target. The stop is wide and fixed, several times the target: 2 x the mean H1 range is a scale-free reading of the vendors' fixed per-pair stops of 37-60 pips. Positions close at 21:00 New York, following Keep Sleeping, which takes entries until 20:00 and closes at 21:00. Other common elements: a spread cap (almost universal), one position per pair, Monday-Thursday only, Christmas to New Year and US holidays off.

Where the sources disagree, the alternatives became the six neighbours:
- the first hour only (SFE's core hour);
- a middle-band target that moves with price (Black Night, PolyFit);
- wider bands;
- M15 bars;
- a wider stop;
- one trade per night (article 1373, NoCap).

The basket is all twelve crosses fixed in dl_round6.py, so nothing is chosen. Majors are left out so that every pair has the same data source and the same cost model, and because round 3 already covered their hours. The tail nights are kept. The GBP flash crash (2016-10-06, around 19:07 New York) and the JPY/AUD crash (2019-01-02, around 17:40 New York) both fall inside the window.

The cost model uses Dukascopy's real minute quotes plus FP's 6 USD commission, converted at fixed declared rates. FP's spreads on crosses are not verified, and the FP model clips the intraday spread shape at 6x, which would understate the minutes after the rollover. No swap applies because trades never cross 17:00.

Every decision quantity is causal:
- the quote test uses only bars before the first decision;
- the spread reference uses earlier evenings only;
- the H1 range uses completed bars only;
- the bands include the closed signal bar.

All decisions are computed once on the base frame, so the base, costs x1.5 and gross runs hold the same trades. The pass bars are the PROTOCOL_V3 basket bars. As in Addenda B and K, I also kept the PROTOCOL_V2 bars (n, neighbours' median PF, every-signal expectancy, hold). On top come the pooled null, built so random days are only evenings the rule could trade, and the correlation of 0.30 or less with the ten.

The priors are stated without touching any data: a positive gross edge is likely, but a failure on costs is expected, because the lab pays the full spread with market orders where the vendors rest limit orders. The survey's earlier placeholder (M15, 18:30-23:45) is replaced and the reason is stated in the text. It was written before the sources were read and was never declared.
